## Architectural decisions landed this cycle

### 1. Brevity Contract (in `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md`)

- Hard caps: `description` ≤ 200 chars; `intent` ≤ 100 words;
  `outcomes.desired` and `outcomes.undesired` 3-6 items each, ≤ 2
  sentences per item; `## Context` ≤ ~250 words; `## Strategy`
  ~7 decisions, ~80 words each; `## Good examples` and `## Bad examples`
  2-3 each; whole-file target ~150 lines, refactor signal at ~175.
- **Forbidden content (anywhere in `.aide`):** type signatures; file
  paths and folder layouts; argument-index or position-based contracts;
  schema cardinality assertions; migration history, deprecation notes,
  retired prior designs; long justifications/asides annotating the
  contract; function-name enumerations standing in for outcomes.
- **Domain vocabulary IS allowed:** entity names, status enum values,
  field names that name a domain concept, domain enum values, specific
  numbers tied to domain decisions.

### 2. Body sections are conditional (Phase 2 deepening)

- Default: spec is **frontmatter-only** — a navigation stub that names
  the module's intent and outcomes. Most modules stay here.
- Body sections appear only when the orchestrator routes through
  synthesize. Synthesize runs only when:
  - The spec-writer signals "Strategy needed: yes" during Stage 1
  - OR the user explicitly asks for synthesis ("synthesize", "fill the
    body")
- All-or-nothing: when body exists, all five sections (Context,
  Strategy, Good examples, Bad examples, References) are present.
  Partial bodies are forbidden.
- When filled, body sections persist — they are the durable domain
  contract, not ephemeral. Unlike `plan.aide` / `todo.aide` /
  `brief.aide` (deleted post-QA), body sections survive the maintainer
  pass.

### 3. Stage 1 returns two routing signals

The spec-writer's Return Format now carries:

- `Research needed: yes/no` — gates Stage 2
- `Strategy needed: yes/no` — gates Stage 3

The orchestrator's routing decision table maps the four signal
combinations to the next stage. Default expectation is "no, no" →
straight to Stage 4 (Plan). The orchestrator passes the user's
implementation context (gathered during the Stage 1 interview)
verbatim into the architect's delegation prompt for Shape B plans.

### 4. Stage 4 splits into two shapes

- **Shape A — synthesize ran.** `.aide` has body sections; sibling
  `brief.aide` exists. Architect reads both; UPDATES `brief.aide` as
  planning surfaces commitments.
- **Shape B — synthesize skipped.** `.aide` is frontmatter-only; no
  `brief.aide`. Architect plans from frontmatter + user implementation
  context (in delegation prompt) + coding playbook. CREATES `brief.aide`
  only if planning surfaces commitments worth recording (most simple
  Shape-B modules don't need one).

### 5. Ephemeral artifacts (unchanged from Apr 29)

- `plan.aide` — deleted by maintainer agent after QA passes.
- `todo.aide` — deleted by maintainer after retro promotion to brain.
- `brief.aide` — deleted by maintainer post-QA when present.
- Code + git history are the durable artifacts.

### 6. Maintainer agent — STILL DEFERRED

Will own deletion of `brief.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide` post-QA.
Trigger and scope still TBD (probably `/aide:maintain`, user-invoked).
Not yet defined as an agent file or command. The orchestrator's
Completion step continues to surface the cleanup list to the user.

### 7. Two new file types (carried from Apr 29)

- **`brief.aide`** (per-module). Architect's pre-read. Holds
  implementation contracts kicked out of `.aide` by the Brevity
  Contract — type shapes, exact strings, schema details, cross-module
  contracts, open questions, mid-flight rework state.
  - **Strategist authors during synthesize** (Shape A only).
  - **Architect creates during plan** (Shape B only, when commitments
    surface).
  - Implementor reads during build; QA reads during qa; deleted
    post-QA by maintainer.
  - Doc: `.aide/docs/brief-aide.md`.
- **`session.aide`** (root only at `.aide/session.aide`). This file.
  Replaces the legacy `handoff.aide` pattern. Survives across the
  feature lifecycle; deleted only when the feature is fully complete.
  Doc: `.aide/docs/session-aide.md`.

### 8. `.aide/handoff.aide` is retired

Deleted in Phase 1 commit. Contents have been absorbed into this
file's anti-regression invariants below.

